# How this version's notes were made (W3C PROV-O). The codes and reason phrases are those of v1 and v2.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T17:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-status-codes> ,
              <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6585> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4918> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5842> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2518> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8297> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8470> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7725> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3229> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2295> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2774> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2324> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "Notes. Each card's back note, under 150 characters, says in plain words how the status is used in practice: when a server sends it, what a client does with it, and the headers that go with it. The notes were written in this deck's own words from the RFCs that define each code, as the IANA registry names them, and then checked against those RFCs and MDN's reference by a separate fact-checking pass, which corrected four of them (100, 204, 302, 403). No text was taken from MDN or the RFCs, so the deck stays CC0."@en .

